SUMMARY:CFAR Health Access SWG: Language Justice
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261015T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261015T180000
DTSTAMP:20261008T061648Z
UID:00ffad471ed2028d@gid-seminars.wnprc.wisc.edu
CATEGORIES:Fred Hutch Center for AIDS Research (CFAR)
DESCRIPTION:Event interval: Single day event\nOnline Meeting Link: https:/
 /washington.zoom.us/meeting/register/K7Dkwt-2R82qj3ZKU18mCw\nAccessibility
  Contact: g0201@uw.edu\nEvent Types: Lectures/Seminars\n\nRegister here!: 
 \nhttps://washington.zoom.us/meeting/register/K7Dkwt-2R82qj3ZKU18mCw\n\nIn
 creasing Participation of Spanish Speakers with Limited English Proficienc
 y (LEP) in HIV Clinical Research in the U.S.\n\nLatinx individuals account
  for nearly 30% of new HIV infections in the United States yet comprise le
 ss than 12% of participants in HIV clinical trials. To evaluate the struct
 ural mechanisms underlying this disparity\, a qualitative needs assessment
  was conducted evaluating barriers\, language access practices\, and insti
 tutional frameworks across clinical research sites (CRSs) in the country a
 ffiliated with the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (
 NIAID)-funded networks\, including the HIV Vaccine Trials Network (HVTN)\,
  HIV Prevention Trials Network (HPTN)\, and Adolescent Medicine Trials Net
 work (ATN). This webinar will present the preliminary results of this rese
 arch contrasting ideal federal aspirations—such as the revised NIH Polic
 y and Guidelines on the Inclusion of Women and Minorities as Subjects in C
 linical Research of July 2025\, the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) Dec
 ember 2025 Guidance on broadening eligibility criteria\, and the statutory
  protections under Title VI of the Civil Rights Act—against the operatio
 nal field realities reported by CRS’s research personnel.\n\nSpeakers:\n
 Pedro Goicochea\, MSc\, MA is a public health independent researcher with 
 decades of experience in communications\, HIV prevention\, clinical resear
 ch\, and community engagement across the United States and Latin America. 
 Originally from Peru\, he relocated to the U.S. in 2008 to contribute to t
 he landmark iPrEx study—the first clinical trial to demonstrate the effi
 cacy of pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) in preventing HIV among men and tr
 ansgender women. Since the early years of the HIV\n\nEvent URL: https://wa
 shington.zoom.us/meeting/register/K7Dkwt-2R82qj3ZKU18mCw\n\nSource: uw_cfa
 r
LOCATION:Online
URL:https://washington.zoom.us/meeting/register/K7Dkwt-2R82qj3ZKU18mCw
BEGIN:VALARM
ACTION:DISPLAY
DESCRIPTION:Reminder: CFAR Health Access SWG: Language Justice
TRIGGER:-PT1H
END:VALARM
END:VEVENT
